    "presentation": {
      "chief_complaint": "Fever and fatigue",
      "hpi": "An 82-year-old man is admitted with one week of fever and fatigue. He has no prosthetic valve. A dental examination shows several caries and periodontal disease. Home medicines are lisinopril 10 mg daily and atorvastatin 40 mg daily. Admission temperature is 38.8 C. Two blood-culture sets drawn before antibiotics grew Streptococcus mitis group, reported susceptible to penicillin. Ceftriaxone 2 g intravenously once daily was given after those cultures were drawn. Creatinine was 1.6 mg/dL on hospital day 1, above an outpatient baseline of 0.9 mg/dL, while he was febrile and eating poorly. Lisinopril was not administered that day. Repeat blood cultures on hospital day 3 showed no growth. A transesophageal echocardiogram shows an 8 mm mitral vegetation, mild mitral regurgitation, no abscess, and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 55 percent. Cardiac surgery found no current indication for an operation: he has no heart-failure symptoms from the valve lesion, no abscess, and the bacteremia has cleared. Discharge creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL. Lisinopril 10 mg was administered the day before discharge, and the blood pressure remained in the 130s systolic. A peripherally inserted central catheter is in place. Weekly blood count and creatinine are arranged.",
      "review_of_systems": null,
      "presenting_symptoms": [
        "Fever",
        "Fatigue"
      ],
      "symptom_duration": "one week",
      "symptom_course": "fever resolved after antibiotics"
